# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the rawform UI app.
#
# The PROVEN floor of leak suppressions is baked into the sanitizer builds
# (utils/SanitizerSuppressions.cpp, __lsan_default_suppressions), because
# env-var delivery through IDE run configurations proved fragile. This file
# is the no-rebuild STAGING overlay for triage, added ON TOP of the baked
# list when pointed at through the environment of the RUN (not the build):
#
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=/path/to/rawform/ui/tools/lsan.supp
#
# An entry that proves out here graduates into SanitizerSuppressions.cpp.
#
# ADMISSION CRITERIA, strict by design (same bar as tsan.supp): a pattern
# lands here only when every frame of the reported allocation stack sits in
# third-party code (Qt platform plugins, system libraries) AND the leak is a
# process-lifetime singleton the library never frees on exit, so it cannot
# implicate rawform code and cannot grow over a session. A leak that recurs
# per action (per file, per track, per dialog open) does NOT qualify, however
# third-party its stack looks: growth means a real bug somewhere.

# libxkbcommon keymap/compose state, allocated once by the Qt Wayland platform
# plugin (QWaylandDisplay::setupConnection and the text-input compose table
# the first time a text field takes a key). Held for the life of the process
# and not released by the Wayland client teardown path; reported at exit.
# Typing in any text field "triggers" the compose-table half, which is why it
# first surfaced against the rename dialog's pattern field; the field is
# incidental. Graduated into SanitizerSuppressions.cpp; kept here so a run
# with a stale binary still has it.
leak:libxkbcommon
